1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,775
You ask a question, and a moment later the answer starts streaming back, one

2
00:00:04,775 --> 00:00:06,338
token at a time.

3
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:10,500
The model writing it is the result of one of the largest computations people

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:13,125
have ever run.

5
00:00:13,575 --> 00:00:18,100
Here is every notable AI model with a published estimate of its training compute,

6
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:22,700
placed by release date and by how many arithmetic operations it took to train,

7
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:24,625
on a logarithmic scale.

8
00:00:24,625 --> 00:00:26,812
GPT-2, in 2019:

9
00:00:26,812 --> 00:00:29,400
about ten to the twenty-one operations.

10
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:31,412
GPT-3, a year later:

11
00:00:31,412 --> 00:00:33,225
more than a hundred times that.

12
00:00:33,225 --> 00:00:34,463
GPT-4:

13
00:00:34,463 --> 00:00:36,983
about seventy times more again.

14
00:00:36,983 --> 00:00:43,708
The largest runs of 2025 and 2026 are estimated at ten to the twenty-six operations

15
00:00:43,708 --> 00:00:45,033
and beyond.

16
00:00:45,033 --> 00:00:49,796
At the frontier, training compute has been growing almost five times, every

17
00:00:49,796 --> 00:00:51,442
single year.

18
00:00:51,442 --> 00:00:53,729
And so that none of this is a cartoon:

19
00:00:53,729 --> 00:00:58,367
every experiment in this video was actually run, in miniature, on the four-core

20
00:00:58,367 --> 00:01:00,254
computer that rendered it.

21
00:01:00,254 --> 00:01:04,679
All of our training runs together took less than ten to the fifteen operations,

22
00:01:04,679 --> 00:01:08,267
roughly a hundred billion times less than a frontier run.

23
00:01:08,267 --> 00:01:09,979
The scale is different.

24
00:01:09,979 --> 00:01:13,117
The ideas are the same.

25
00:01:13,675 --> 00:01:17,150
This video follows one of those training runs from end to end.

26
00:01:17,150 --> 00:01:21,487
It starts with a crawl of the public web, which is filtered, deduplicated

27
00:01:21,487 --> 00:01:24,862
and mixed into a dataset of tens of trillions of tokens.

28
00:01:24,862 --> 00:01:28,762
Pretraining teaches a network to predict the next token of that text, with

29
00:01:28,762 --> 00:01:32,462
the work spread across tens of thousands of GPUs for months.

30
00:01:32,462 --> 00:01:36,325
Post-training then turns that raw text predictor into something that follows

31
00:01:36,325 --> 00:01:41,500
instructions and reasons, and the result is the assistant.

32
00:01:42,117 --> 00:01:43,717
We'll go in four parts:

33
00:01:43,717 --> 00:01:48,992
the data, the training recipe, scaling it out across a cluster, and post-training.

34
00:01:48,992 --> 00:01:52,542
Along the way, we'll look at what the labs have actually published about how

35
00:01:52,542 --> 00:01:55,467
they do it, as of October 2026.

36
00:02:02,306 --> 00:02:03,006
Part one:

37
00:02:03,006 --> 00:02:05,256
the data.

38
00:02:06,231 --> 00:02:09,606
Almost every large language model starts from the same place:

39
00:02:09,606 --> 00:02:13,494
Common Crawl, a non-profit that crawls the web every month or two and gives

40
00:02:13,494 --> 00:02:14,906
the result away.

41
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:20,406
Its September 2026 crawl alone holds 2.17 billion web pages, a hundred and

42
00:02:20,406 --> 00:02:24,394
six terabytes compressed, split into a hundred thousand files.

43
00:02:24,394 --> 00:02:26,431
Each square here is one of them.

44
00:02:26,431 --> 00:02:30,869
For this video, we downloaded eight of them, plus the first four hundred megabytes

45
00:02:30,869 --> 00:02:34,781
of the raw HTML behind the first.

46
00:02:35,181 --> 00:02:37,769
Here's one page from the very first file:

47
00:02:37,769 --> 00:02:42,231
a blog post with a recipe for savory custard.

48
00:02:42,373 --> 00:02:44,436
This is what the crawler actually stored:

49
00:02:44,436 --> 00:02:47,398
more than two thousand lines of HTML.

50
00:02:47,398 --> 00:02:50,923
Scripts, style sheets, menus, links, metadata.

51
00:02:50,923 --> 00:02:52,586
And the recipe itself?

52
00:02:52,586 --> 00:02:54,511
All of it sits on a single line:

53
00:02:54,511 --> 00:02:56,673
number 436.

54
00:02:56,673 --> 00:03:00,648
Ninety-eight kilobytes of HTML, for three kilobytes of text:

55
00:03:00,648 --> 00:03:03,423
about three percent.

56
00:03:03,973 --> 00:03:08,036
Across the first ten thousand pages of that file, it's the same story:

57
00:03:08,036 --> 00:03:12,911
1.65 gigabytes of HTML become thirty-seven megabytes of text.

58
00:03:12,911 --> 00:03:15,711
Pulling out the main text is a step of its own.

59
00:03:15,711 --> 00:03:20,473
Common Crawl ships an extracted version, but the FineWeb team found that re-extracting

60
00:03:20,473 --> 00:03:25,448
from the raw HTML, with a library called Trafilatura, leaves out more menus

61
00:03:25,448 --> 00:03:28,273
and boilerplate, and trains better models.

62
00:03:28,273 --> 00:03:30,998
Here's our recipe page both ways.

63
00:03:30,998 --> 00:03:35,198
Common Crawl's version keeps the comments, the cookbook ads, and a hundred-line

64
00:03:35,198 --> 00:03:36,536
blog archive.

65
00:03:36,536 --> 00:03:40,323
Over all the pages that survive our filters, it's twice as long.

66
00:03:40,323 --> 00:03:42,236
And the web is multilingual:

67
00:03:42,236 --> 00:03:46,073
in this sample, only four pages in ten are in English.

68
00:03:46,073 --> 00:03:48,561
Frontier models train on many languages;

69
00:03:48,561 --> 00:03:51,698
here, like the original FineWeb, we'll keep English.

70
00:03:53,888 --> 00:03:55,388
Next, filtering.

71
00:03:55,388 --> 00:04:00,226
We took the exact recipe behind FineWeb, Hugging Face's open web dataset of

72
00:04:00,226 --> 00:04:05,276
fifteen trillion tokens, and ran their own code on those ten thousand pages.

73
00:04:05,276 --> 00:04:08,397
Here's how many pages survive each step.

74
00:04:08,397 --> 00:04:12,734
First, a blocklist of four and a half million domains removes adult sites,

75
00:04:12,734 --> 00:04:14,509
malware and spam.

76
00:04:14,509 --> 00:04:19,172
Pages with no extractable text go next, like this one, which does nothing

77
00:04:19,172 --> 00:04:21,072
but redirect your browser.

78
00:04:21,072 --> 00:04:25,409
Then a language classifier keeps only pages that are confidently English:

79
00:04:25,409 --> 00:04:27,638
about a third of what's left.

80
00:04:27,638 --> 00:04:29,851
Then come rules written by hand.

81
00:04:29,851 --> 00:04:34,213
Repetition rules, from DeepMind's Gopher paper, drop pages where too many

82
00:04:34,213 --> 00:04:38,663
lines, paragraphs or phrases repeat, like this forum index:

83
00:04:38,663 --> 00:04:43,647
zero replies, last post by Archive, over and over.

84
00:04:43,647 --> 00:04:48,272
Quality rules drop pages with fewer than fifty words, or where too few words

85
00:04:48,272 --> 00:04:52,384
contain a letter, or that lack ordinary words like the and and.

86
00:04:52,384 --> 00:04:55,163
Like this login form.

87
00:04:55,163 --> 00:05:00,338
Rules from Google's C4 dataset drop lines that don't end in punctuation, and

88
00:05:00,338 --> 00:05:03,751
any page with lorem ipsum or a curly bracket in it:

89
00:05:03,751 --> 00:05:06,051
a sign of code or templates.

90
00:05:06,051 --> 00:05:12,047
This page is spun advertising text with its template syntax still showing.

91
00:05:12,047 --> 00:05:16,334
And FineWeb added three rules of its own, chosen by training small models

92
00:05:16,334 --> 00:05:17,509
on the output:

93
00:05:17,509 --> 00:05:22,584
drop pages where few lines end in punctuation, where most lines are very short,

94
00:05:22,584 --> 00:05:24,647
or where many lines repeat.

95
00:05:24,647 --> 00:05:28,005
Menus and navigation, mostly.

96
00:05:28,005 --> 00:05:29,718
These rules are blunt.

97
00:05:29,718 --> 00:05:33,930
This page, a programming puzzle full of symbols, was thrown out for having

98
00:05:33,930 --> 00:05:35,630
too few real words.

99
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:38,218
At web scale that's an acceptable trade:

100
00:05:38,218 --> 00:05:43,530
losing some good pages costs far less than training on bad ones.

101
00:05:43,697 --> 00:05:47,834
In the end, thirteen percent of the pages survive, holding about a third of

102
00:05:47,834 --> 00:05:50,047
a percent of the original bytes.

103
00:05:50,047 --> 00:05:55,184
FineWeb ran this, plus deduplication, over ninety-six crawls, and ended up

104
00:05:55,184 --> 00:05:57,147
with fifteen trillion tokens.

105
00:05:59,402 --> 00:06:01,215
The web is full of copies:

106
00:06:01,215 --> 00:06:04,590
mirrored sites, templates, boilerplate pages.

107
00:06:04,590 --> 00:06:09,890
Training on the same text over and over wastes compute and encourages memorization.

108
00:06:09,890 --> 00:06:13,390
Here's the biggest cluster of near-duplicates in our eight files:

109
00:06:13,390 --> 00:06:18,727
forty-one pages announcing that some domain name is for sale.

110
00:06:19,194 --> 00:06:23,919
To measure how alike two pages are, cut each one into overlapping five-word

111
00:06:23,919 --> 00:06:24,957
shingles.

112
00:06:24,957 --> 00:06:28,919
The Jaccard similarity is the number of shingles they share, divided by the

113
00:06:28,919 --> 00:06:31,257
number of distinct shingles in either.

114
00:06:31,257 --> 00:06:35,894
For these two pages, it's 0.78.

115
00:06:36,286 --> 00:06:39,486
But comparing every page with every other is hopeless.

116
00:06:39,486 --> 00:06:43,086
Billions of pages means billions of billions of pairs.

117
00:06:43,086 --> 00:06:45,973
MinHash gets around this with a beautiful trick.

118
00:06:45,973 --> 00:06:50,111
Run every shingle of a page through a random hash function, and keep only

119
00:06:50,111 --> 00:06:51,911
the smallest value.

120
00:06:51,911 --> 00:06:55,848
Two pages get the same minimum exactly when the smallest-hashed shingle of

121
00:06:55,848 --> 00:07:00,511
the two pages combined is one they share, which happens with probability equal

122
00:07:00,511 --> 00:07:02,711
to their Jaccard similarity.

123
00:07:02,711 --> 00:07:08,248
Repeat with 112 different hash functions, and each page gets a short fingerprint.

124
00:07:08,248 --> 00:07:12,473
For our two pages, 86 of the 112 minimums agree:

125
00:07:12,473 --> 00:07:16,561
an estimate of 0.77.

126
00:07:17,827 --> 00:07:20,915
Then, instead of comparing fingerprints, hash them.

127
00:07:20,915 --> 00:07:24,390
Cut the 112 numbers into 14 bands of eight.

128
00:07:24,390 --> 00:07:28,352
Pages that match on all eight numbers of any band land in the same bucket,

129
00:07:28,352 --> 00:07:30,090
and become candidates.

130
00:07:30,090 --> 00:07:34,090
The chance of that, as a function of similarity, is this S-curve:

131
00:07:34,090 --> 00:07:38,040
pages that are eighty percent alike are caught ninety-two percent of the time;

132
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:42,477
pages that are half alike, about five percent.

133
00:07:42,961 --> 00:07:46,761
In our eight files, deduplication removed about three percent of the pages

134
00:07:46,761 --> 00:07:50,161
that survived filtering, in 114 clusters:

135
00:07:50,161 --> 00:07:55,023
domain-for-sale pages, access-denied pages, default web-hosting pages.

136
00:07:55,023 --> 00:07:59,111
Across a hundred crawls, the same pages turn up again and again, so the stakes

137
00:07:59,111 --> 00:08:02,023
are much higher, and FineWeb found a surprise.

138
00:08:02,023 --> 00:08:05,711
Deduplicating across all the crawls threw away ninety percent of the oldest

139
00:08:05,711 --> 00:08:08,798
ones, and what remained trained worse models.

140
00:08:08,798 --> 00:08:11,811
So FineWeb deduplicates each crawl separately.

141
00:08:13,967 --> 00:08:17,792
Hand-written rules catch obvious junk, but they can't tell whether a page

142
00:08:17,792 --> 00:08:19,367
is worth learning from.

143
00:08:19,367 --> 00:08:21,905
For that, labs use models.

144
00:08:21,905 --> 00:08:26,867
FineWeb-Edu asked Llama 3 70B to grade half a million pages for educational

145
00:08:26,867 --> 00:08:31,767
value, from zero to five, trained a small, fast classifier to imitate those

146
00:08:31,767 --> 00:08:35,780
grades, and ran it over all fifteen trillion tokens.

147
00:08:35,780 --> 00:08:39,792
Then it kept the pages scoring three or more.

148
00:08:40,234 --> 00:08:44,309
We ran that same classifier on four hundred random pages that survived our

149
00:08:44,309 --> 00:08:45,434
filters.

150
00:08:45,434 --> 00:08:46,709
Most score low:

151
00:08:46,709 --> 00:08:49,172
shops, services, local news.

152
00:08:49,172 --> 00:08:53,622
Keeping three and up, as FineWeb-Edu does, leaves just seven percent.

153
00:08:53,622 --> 00:08:58,934
The top scorer looks like this, and a typical low scorer like this.

154
00:08:59,342 --> 00:09:04,667
At full scale, FineWeb-Edu kept a similar eight percent of FineWeb, 1.3 trillion

155
00:09:04,667 --> 00:09:09,192
tokens, and models trained on it matched older datasets on knowledge benchmarks

156
00:09:09,192 --> 00:09:11,542
with ten times fewer tokens.

157
00:09:11,542 --> 00:09:15,817
Since then, the trend is to rewrite rather than throw away.

158
00:09:15,817 --> 00:09:20,630
NVIDIA's Nemotron-CC used a language model to rephrase web pages, producing

159
00:09:20,630 --> 00:09:23,842
almost two trillion synthetic tokens.

160
00:09:23,842 --> 00:09:29,042
Moonshot rephrased Kimi K2's knowledge data, and a factual-recall score rose

161
00:09:29,042 --> 00:09:31,492
from 24 to 29 percent.

162
00:09:31,492 --> 00:09:36,767
And AI2's OLMo 3 repeats its best data up to seven times, instead of drawing

163
00:09:36,767 --> 00:09:39,317
a hard line.

164
00:09:39,792 --> 00:09:41,455
Then the data is mixed.

165
00:09:41,455 --> 00:09:46,205
Llama 3's recipe was about half general knowledge, a quarter math and reasoning,

166
00:09:46,205 --> 00:09:49,817
seventeen percent code, and eight percent other languages.

167
00:09:49,817 --> 00:09:54,217
And in the last stretch of training, the mix shifts toward the very best data.

168
00:09:54,217 --> 00:09:58,892
Annealing Llama 3's 8-billion-parameter model this way raised its score on

169
00:09:58,892 --> 00:10:03,342
grade-school math problems by twenty-four percent.

170
00:10:03,776 --> 00:10:05,888
The finished datasets are enormous:

171
00:10:05,888 --> 00:10:11,188
around fifteen trillion tokens for Llama 3, DeepSeek-V3 and Kimi K2;

172
00:10:11,188 --> 00:10:14,063
twenty-eight and a half trillion for GLM-5;

173
00:10:14,063 --> 00:10:18,863
thirty-three trillion for DeepSeek-V4, released in April 2026;

174
00:10:18,863 --> 00:10:21,526
thirty-six trillion for Qwen3.

175
00:10:21,526 --> 00:10:24,376
Last, the text becomes tokens.

176
00:10:24,376 --> 00:10:30,163
A tokenizer, trained with byte-pair encoding, breaks text into frequent pieces;

177
00:10:30,163 --> 00:10:35,776
frontier tokenizers have between 128 and 260 thousand of them.

178
00:10:35,776 --> 00:10:42,251
We trained a small one, with 2,048, and used it to encode 168 million tokens

179
00:10:42,251 --> 00:10:43,751
of FineWeb-Edu:

180
00:10:43,751 --> 00:10:46,451
the fuel for the training runs in the next part.

181
00:10:48,474 --> 00:10:49,124
Part two:

182
00:10:49,124 --> 00:10:51,574
the recipe.

183
00:10:52,508 --> 00:10:56,720
Before training anything, a lab has to decide how big the model should be.

184
00:10:56,720 --> 00:10:59,583
The budget is compute, and there's a simple rule for it:

185
00:10:59,583 --> 00:11:03,070
training costs about six times the number of parameters, times the number

186
00:11:03,070 --> 00:11:04,308
of tokens.

187
00:11:04,308 --> 00:11:08,345
Two operations per parameter per token on the forward pass, a multiply and

188
00:11:08,345 --> 00:11:11,270
an add, and about four more on the backward pass.

189
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:13,383
So a fixed budget is a fixed area:

190
00:11:13,383 --> 00:11:17,283
you can spend it on a big model trained on fewer tokens, or a small model

191
00:11:17,283 --> 00:11:18,595
trained on many.

192
00:11:18,595 --> 00:11:21,133
Which is better?

193
00:11:21,574 --> 00:11:24,662
The way to find out is an experiment, so we ran one.

194
00:11:24,662 --> 00:11:28,687
Pick a compute budget, and train models of several sizes, each on exactly

195
00:11:28,687 --> 00:11:30,962
the number of tokens the budget allows.

196
00:11:30,962 --> 00:11:34,087
Plot final loss against model size, and you get a valley:

197
00:11:34,087 --> 00:11:37,524
too small a model can't hold what it sees, too large a model doesn't get to

198
00:11:37,524 --> 00:11:38,512
see enough.

199
00:11:38,512 --> 00:11:42,274
Repeat at four budgets, twenty-five runs in all, and fit a parabola to each

200
00:11:42,274 --> 00:11:43,512
valley floor.

201
00:11:43,512 --> 00:11:47,612
The best size grows with the budget, as a power law, with an exponent of about

202
00:11:47,612 --> 00:11:49,174
three quarters.

203
00:11:49,174 --> 00:11:53,787
That's close to what OpenAI found in 2020 with small models.

204
00:11:53,787 --> 00:11:57,937
The much larger study we'll see next found about one half, and later work

205
00:11:57,937 --> 00:12:02,412
traced the gap to details of how very small models like ours are measured

206
00:12:02,412 --> 00:12:04,824
and tuned.

207
00:12:05,266 --> 00:12:09,616
In 2022, DeepMind's Chinchilla paper did this with more than four hundred

208
00:12:09,616 --> 00:12:13,941
models, up to sixteen billion parameters, and fitted a formula for the loss

209
00:12:13,941 --> 00:12:16,479
as a function of model size and data.

210
00:12:16,479 --> 00:12:18,391
Here is the landscape it describes;

211
00:12:18,391 --> 00:12:20,404
darker means lower loss.

212
00:12:20,404 --> 00:12:23,204
Each yellow line is a fixed compute budget.

213
00:12:23,204 --> 00:12:27,154
Along each one there's a best point, and together they trace this path:

214
00:12:27,154 --> 00:12:32,366
grow parameters and tokens together, about twenty tokens for every parameter.

215
00:12:32,366 --> 00:12:37,916
By that measure, GPT-3 had been trained on far too little data, and the 70-billion

216
00:12:37,916 --> 00:12:43,379
parameter Chinchilla, trained on 1.4 trillion tokens, beat a model four times

217
00:12:43,379 --> 00:12:45,741
its size.

218
00:12:46,174 --> 00:12:48,674
But nobody actually stops at twenty.

219
00:12:48,674 --> 00:12:53,174
Llama 3's team used the same kind of IsoFLOP experiment, up to ten to the

220
00:12:53,174 --> 00:12:58,899
twenty-two operations, to choose the size of their 405-billion-parameter model,

221
00:12:58,899 --> 00:13:01,812
and then trained their small models far longer:

222
00:13:01,812 --> 00:13:07,049
the 8-billion-parameter Llama 3 saw fifteen trillion tokens, almost nineteen

223
00:13:07,049 --> 00:13:10,324
hundred per parameter, and it kept improving.

224
00:13:10,324 --> 00:13:12,687
The reason is cost after training:

225
00:13:12,687 --> 00:13:16,649
a smaller model trained for longer is much cheaper to run for millions of

226
00:13:16,649 --> 00:13:17,699
people.

227
00:13:17,699 --> 00:13:22,274
Frontier mixtures of experts land in between, at hundreds to over a thousand

228
00:13:22,274 --> 00:13:24,374
tokens per active parameter.

229
00:13:26,406 --> 00:13:27,469
What gets trained?

230
00:13:27,469 --> 00:13:28,831
Still a transformer:

231
00:13:28,831 --> 00:13:33,106
the same basic blueprint as GPT-2, which our earlier video took apart piece

232
00:13:33,106 --> 00:13:34,181
by piece.

233
00:13:34,181 --> 00:13:36,669
But nearly every component has been refined:

234
00:13:36,669 --> 00:13:41,806
a simpler normalization, rotary position embeddings, gated MLPs, attention

235
00:13:41,806 --> 00:13:46,394
that shares or compresses its keys and values to save memory, and normalization

236
00:13:46,394 --> 00:13:48,831
of the queries and keys for stability.

237
00:13:48,831 --> 00:13:53,119
Our pocket models use the first three, and QK-norm gets an experiment of its

238
00:13:53,119 --> 00:13:55,731
own later on.

239
00:13:56,140 --> 00:14:00,527
The biggest architectural change of the last two years is the mixture of experts.

240
00:14:00,527 --> 00:14:04,965
In a standard transformer, every token passes through the whole MLP in every

241
00:14:04,965 --> 00:14:06,027
layer.

242
00:14:06,027 --> 00:14:10,690
A mixture-of-experts layer replaces that one MLP with many smaller ones, the

243
00:14:10,690 --> 00:14:14,990
experts, and a small router that scores them for each token and sends the

244
00:14:14,990 --> 00:14:17,077
token to only the top few.

245
00:14:17,077 --> 00:14:21,140
So the number of parameters grows with the number of experts, while the computation

246
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:25,365
per token grows only with the number chosen.

247
00:14:25,823 --> 00:14:28,348
Frontier open models push this hard.

248
00:14:28,348 --> 00:14:34,273
DeepSeek-V3 has 256 experts per layer and uses eight of them, plus one shared

249
00:14:34,273 --> 00:14:36,648
expert that every token visits:

250
00:14:36,648 --> 00:14:42,323
671 billion parameters in total, but only 37 billion doing work for any one

251
00:14:42,323 --> 00:14:43,436
token.

252
00:14:43,436 --> 00:14:49,323
Kimi K2 has 384 experts and passes a trillion parameters.

253
00:14:49,323 --> 00:14:57,661
And Kimi K3, from July 2026, picks sixteen of 896, for 2.8 trillion parameters.

254
00:14:57,661 --> 00:15:01,686
Experts keep getting more numerous, and the fraction used per token keeps

255
00:15:01,686 --> 00:15:06,123
shrinking, now around three to five percent.

256
00:15:06,540 --> 00:15:08,602
But routers have a failure mode.

257
00:15:08,602 --> 00:15:12,465
Experts that get more tokens improve faster and get chosen even more:

258
00:15:12,465 --> 00:15:13,827
the rich get richer.

259
00:15:13,827 --> 00:15:17,777
Here are three real runs of our pocket mixture of experts, eight experts,

260
00:15:17,777 --> 00:15:19,102
two per token.

261
00:15:19,102 --> 00:15:22,715
Watch the share of tokens each expert receives as training goes.

262
00:15:22,715 --> 00:15:27,215
With no balancing, the busiest expert ends up with three times its fair share,

263
00:15:27,215 --> 00:15:29,340
while another gets almost nothing.

264
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:34,340
The classic fix is an extra loss term that penalizes uneven routing.

265
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:36,952
DeepSeek-V3 used a gentler trick:

266
00:15:36,952 --> 00:15:41,465
a bias added to each expert's score, only for choosing experts, nudged up

267
00:15:41,465 --> 00:15:45,490
after every step if the expert was underused and down if it was overused,

268
00:15:45,490 --> 00:15:48,565
with no extra loss term competing with learning.

269
00:15:48,565 --> 00:15:51,327
Here, it balances almost perfectly.

270
00:15:51,327 --> 00:15:55,465
In our tiny runs, the auxiliary loss still finished with a slightly lower

271
00:15:55,465 --> 00:15:56,252
loss;

272
00:15:56,252 --> 00:16:02,290
in DeepSeek's experiments, at billions of parameters, the bias came out ahead.

273
00:16:02,965 --> 00:16:07,215
A few other shifts are reshaping the blueprint, mostly to make long contexts

274
00:16:07,215 --> 00:16:08,640
and inference cheaper.

275
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:13,277
Sparse attention lets each token look at only its most relevant earlier tokens.

276
00:16:13,277 --> 00:16:17,315
Hybrid models replace three of every four attention layers with cheaper linear-time

277
00:16:17,315 --> 00:16:18,352
layers.

278
00:16:18,352 --> 00:16:22,477
Multi-token prediction adds heads that guess several tokens ahead, both as

279
00:16:22,477 --> 00:16:25,940
extra training signal and to speed up generation.

280
00:16:25,940 --> 00:16:30,365
And context windows of a million tokens are now common, reached in stages

281
00:16:30,365 --> 00:16:31,640
near the end of training.

282
00:16:33,747 --> 00:16:35,322
Next, the optimizer:

283
00:16:35,322 --> 00:16:38,209
the rule that turns each gradient into an update.

284
00:16:38,209 --> 00:16:42,559
Plain gradient descent steps straight downhill, and in a long, narrow valley

285
00:16:42,559 --> 00:16:46,472
it zigzags across the steep walls while creeping along the floor.

286
00:16:46,472 --> 00:16:49,309
Adam fixes this one coordinate at a time:

287
00:16:49,309 --> 00:16:53,472
it keeps running averages of each parameter's gradient and squared gradient,

288
00:16:53,472 --> 00:16:57,509
and divides one by the square root of the other, so every coordinate moves

289
00:16:57,509 --> 00:16:59,372
at a similar speed.

290
00:16:59,372 --> 00:17:05,747
With decoupled weight decay, AdamW has been the default for most of a decade.

291
00:17:06,222 --> 00:17:10,409
A newer optimizer, Muon, treats each weight matrix as a whole.

292
00:17:10,409 --> 00:17:14,784
A matrix's gradient can be broken into directions, its singular vectors, each

293
00:17:14,784 --> 00:17:16,009
with a size.

294
00:17:16,009 --> 00:17:19,447
Here are the sizes for a real gradient from one of our pocket models, on a

295
00:17:19,447 --> 00:17:20,684
log scale.

296
00:17:20,684 --> 00:17:22,822
A handful of directions dominate:

297
00:17:22,822 --> 00:17:28,022
the top five, out of 128, carry about two thirds of it, while a typical direction

298
00:17:28,022 --> 00:17:31,122
is some fifty times smaller than the largest.

299
00:17:31,122 --> 00:17:34,609
Muon keeps the directions but sets every size to one:

300
00:17:34,609 --> 00:17:38,122
each direction gets an equal step.

301
00:17:38,538 --> 00:17:42,888
Computing that exactly would need a singular value decomposition, which is

302
00:17:42,888 --> 00:17:44,963
slow on GPUs.

303
00:17:44,963 --> 00:17:47,526
Muon's trick is a polynomial.

304
00:17:47,526 --> 00:17:52,063
Multiplying a matrix by itself in this pattern applies the same polynomial

305
00:17:52,063 --> 00:17:56,888
to every singular value at once, and the coefficients are chosen so that five

306
00:17:56,888 --> 00:17:59,813
rounds push every value toward one.

307
00:17:59,813 --> 00:18:04,513
Here are the real singular values of our gradient, scaled below one, going

308
00:18:04,513 --> 00:18:06,401
through five iterations:

309
00:18:06,401 --> 00:18:10,901
small ones are lifted fast, any that overshoot are pulled back, and all of

310
00:18:10,901 --> 00:18:16,013
them end up between about 0.6 and 1.2.

311
00:18:16,463 --> 00:18:17,263
Does it help?

312
00:18:17,263 --> 00:18:21,488
Here's our pocket model trained twice on the same data, once with AdamW and

313
00:18:21,488 --> 00:18:25,288
once with Muon, each with the better of the learning rates we tried in short

314
00:18:25,288 --> 00:18:26,501
test runs.

315
00:18:26,501 --> 00:18:29,026
Muon pulls ahead early and stays ahead.

316
00:18:29,026 --> 00:18:32,988
While the learning rate is held at its peak, Muon reaches any given loss with

317
00:18:32,988 --> 00:18:36,363
less than two thirds of the tokens AdamW needs.

318
00:18:36,363 --> 00:18:40,688
At the end, both learning rates are lowered and both losses drop, which is

319
00:18:40,688 --> 00:18:42,988
the subject of the next chapter.

320
00:18:42,988 --> 00:18:48,738
Muon finishes at 3.61, AdamW at 3.80.

321
00:18:49,222 --> 00:18:54,784
Muon began in 2024 as a trick for a community speedrun of small GPT models.

322
00:18:54,784 --> 00:18:59,534
In 2025, Moonshot showed it scales, needing about half the training compute

323
00:18:59,534 --> 00:19:03,972
of AdamW, and trained the trillion-parameter Kimi K2 with it.

324
00:19:03,972 --> 00:19:11,872
By 2026, Zhipu's GLM models, DeepSeek-V4 and Kimi K3 all use Muon variants.

325
00:19:11,872 --> 00:19:13,034
One caveat:

326
00:19:13,034 --> 00:19:18,547
a careful 2025 benchmark found the speed-up shrinks as models grow, from 1.4

327
00:19:18,547 --> 00:19:23,997
times at a hundred million parameters to 1.1 times at 1.2 billion.

328
00:19:23,997 --> 00:19:29,059
Our tiny model's 1.6 fits that trend, though our tuning was far lighter.

329
00:19:29,059 --> 00:19:32,922
Optimizer gains are real, but they're hard to measure at scale.

330
00:19:34,962 --> 00:19:38,787
The learning rate sets how big each step is, and how it changes over the run

331
00:19:38,787 --> 00:19:40,062
matters a lot.

332
00:19:40,062 --> 00:19:44,224
Runs begin with a short warmup, ramping up from near zero, because big steps

333
00:19:44,224 --> 00:19:47,149
on a freshly initialized network can blow it up.

334
00:19:47,149 --> 00:19:48,762
Then the rate decays.

335
00:19:48,762 --> 00:19:52,524
The classic choice is a cosine curve, down to a tenth of the peak.

336
00:19:52,524 --> 00:19:53,962
Cosine has a catch:

337
00:19:53,962 --> 00:19:56,662
you must choose the length of the run in advance.

338
00:19:56,662 --> 00:19:59,987
Stop halfway, and the rate was never brought down.

339
00:19:59,987 --> 00:20:02,974
The alternative is warmup, stable, decay:

340
00:20:02,974 --> 00:20:08,162
hold the peak for most of the run, and decay only in the last fifth.

341
00:20:08,595 --> 00:20:12,332
Here are both schedules on one of our pocket models, with the same data.

342
00:20:12,332 --> 00:20:16,470
During the stable phase, WSD's loss lags slightly behind.

343
00:20:16,470 --> 00:20:21,170
Then its decay begins, and the loss drops off a cliff, finishing below cosine.

344
00:20:21,170 --> 00:20:24,745
Larger published comparisons usually find the two about level.

345
00:20:24,745 --> 00:20:26,945
And the stable phase can be reused:

346
00:20:26,945 --> 00:20:31,282
branch off and decay early, here at forty and sixty percent of the way, and

347
00:20:31,282 --> 00:20:34,445
each branch is a finished model for that budget.

348
00:20:34,445 --> 00:20:38,595
Our half-length branch even beat a separate half-length cosine run.

349
00:20:38,595 --> 00:20:42,870
One long run gives you a model at every budget.

350
00:20:43,328 --> 00:20:45,316
Why does the loss fall so suddenly?

351
00:20:45,316 --> 00:20:46,466
One picture:

352
00:20:46,466 --> 00:20:50,478
late in training, the loss landscape looks like a river valley, with steep

353
00:20:50,478 --> 00:20:53,178
walls and a slowly descending floor.

354
00:20:53,178 --> 00:20:57,203
At a high learning rate, the optimizer bounces between the walls while drifting

355
00:20:57,203 --> 00:20:58,528
downstream.

356
00:20:58,528 --> 00:21:02,541
Lower the rate, and it settles onto the valley floor, revealing progress it

357
00:21:02,541 --> 00:21:05,103
had already made.

358
00:21:05,587 --> 00:21:08,337
Frontier runs use variations of both.

359
00:21:08,337 --> 00:21:12,849
DeepSeek-V3 held its peak learning rate for ten trillion tokens, then decayed

360
00:21:12,849 --> 00:21:14,299
over the next four.

361
00:21:14,299 --> 00:21:19,387
Llama 3 used cosine, annealed to zero over its last forty million tokens,

362
00:21:19,387 --> 00:21:21,124
and averaged checkpoints.

363
00:21:21,124 --> 00:21:25,762
Kimi K3 fitted scaling laws for both schedules and chose cosine.

364
00:21:25,762 --> 00:21:29,987
And the batch size usually grows as training goes, from a few million tokens

365
00:21:29,987 --> 00:21:31,912
per step to tens of millions.

366
00:21:33,986 --> 00:21:37,848
Every number in a training run is stored in a fixed number of bits, and that

367
00:21:37,848 --> 00:21:40,498
choice is one of the biggest levers on speed.

368
00:21:40,498 --> 00:21:44,686
Use half as many bits, and a chip can do roughly twice as many multiplications

369
00:21:44,686 --> 00:21:47,723
per second, while moving half as much memory.

370
00:21:47,723 --> 00:21:52,261
An H100 does about a thousand trillion operations per second in sixteen-bit

371
00:21:52,261 --> 00:21:55,073
bfloat, and twice that in eight-bit.

372
00:21:55,073 --> 00:22:00,661
A Blackwell Ultra chip does fifteen thousand trillion in four-bit.

373
00:22:00,794 --> 00:22:03,106
A floating-point number has three parts:

374
00:22:03,106 --> 00:22:07,794
a sign, an exponent that sets its scale, and a mantissa that sets its precision

375
00:22:07,794 --> 00:22:09,356
within that scale.

376
00:22:09,356 --> 00:22:13,644
Ordinary thirty-two-bit floats have eight exponent bits and twenty-three mantissa

377
00:22:13,644 --> 00:22:14,594
bits.

378
00:22:14,594 --> 00:22:19,594
Bfloat16 keeps all eight exponent bits but only seven of mantissa:

379
00:22:19,594 --> 00:22:21,744
the same range, less precision.

380
00:22:21,744 --> 00:22:24,144
It's the workhorse of training today.

381
00:22:24,144 --> 00:22:29,769
The eight-bit format called E4M3 has four exponent bits and three of mantissa,

382
00:22:29,769 --> 00:22:36,369
and four-bit E2M1 has just two exponent bits and one mantissa bit.

383
00:22:36,527 --> 00:22:40,990
Here is every positive number each format can represent, on a log scale.

384
00:22:40,990 --> 00:22:45,190
FP8 has a hundred and twenty-six of them, from about two thousandths up to

385
00:22:45,190 --> 00:22:46,915
448.

386
00:22:46,915 --> 00:22:48,590
FP4 has seven:

387
00:22:48,590 --> 00:22:52,490
one half, one, one and a half, two, three, four and six.

388
00:22:52,490 --> 00:22:54,827
That's all.

389
00:22:54,936 --> 00:22:58,586
Because the range is so small, low-precision numbers are stored with a scale

390
00:22:58,586 --> 00:22:59,386
factor:

391
00:22:59,386 --> 00:23:02,536
divide by the scale, round, and multiply back.

392
00:23:02,536 --> 00:23:05,223
The question is how many numbers share one scale.

393
00:23:05,223 --> 00:23:06,686
Here is a real tensor:

394
00:23:06,686 --> 00:23:11,161
the residual stream of GPT-2 after six blocks, for one sentence.

395
00:23:11,161 --> 00:23:14,498
Forty-five tokens by 768 channels.

396
00:23:14,498 --> 00:23:16,536
A typical entry is about one.

397
00:23:16,536 --> 00:23:18,561
But a few channels are enormous:

398
00:23:18,561 --> 00:23:22,636
this one reaches almost 2,900 at the first token.

399
00:23:22,636 --> 00:23:26,844
Such massive activations show up in many transformers.

400
00:23:26,844 --> 00:23:30,694
Now store it in FP4 with a single scale for the whole tensor.

401
00:23:30,694 --> 00:23:35,056
The outlier sets the scale, and every ordinary value rounds to zero:

402
00:23:35,056 --> 00:23:38,906
99.99 percent of the tensor simply vanishes.

403
00:23:38,906 --> 00:23:44,019
Give every block of sixteen numbers its own scale, as NVIDIA's NVFP4 format

404
00:23:44,019 --> 00:23:46,456
does, and the picture comes back.

405
00:23:46,456 --> 00:23:50,794
Each value is still rounded coarsely, here with about twelve percent error,

406
00:23:50,794 --> 00:23:53,802
but the information survives.

407
00:23:53,802 --> 00:23:56,765
Zooming in on one token, the word Paris:

408
00:23:56,765 --> 00:24:01,377
each block of sixteen values gets its own scale, stored in eight bits, so

409
00:24:01,377 --> 00:24:06,927
a large value only coarsens its own block, not the whole tensor.

410
00:24:07,061 --> 00:24:10,786
FP8, with its four exponent bits, is more forgiving:

411
00:24:10,786 --> 00:24:15,186
on this same tensor, even a single scale gives under three percent error.

412
00:24:15,186 --> 00:24:18,211
Labs still scale finely, for safety.

413
00:24:18,211 --> 00:24:23,823
DeepSeek-V3 was trained in FP8 with a separate scale for every 128 values,

414
00:24:23,823 --> 00:24:28,911
adding up partial sums in full precision every 128 products, and in tests

415
00:24:28,911 --> 00:24:33,311
against sixteen-bit training its loss stayed within a quarter of a percent.

416
00:24:33,311 --> 00:24:40,611
And in 2026, NVIDIA pretrained Nemotron 3 Ultra, a 550-billion-parameter model,

417
00:24:40,611 --> 00:24:46,561
on twenty trillion tokens in NVFP4, keeping the most sensitive layers in higher

418
00:24:46,561 --> 00:24:51,448
precision and using tricks like random rotations and stochastic rounding to

419
00:24:51,448 --> 00:24:52,861
tame the outliers.

420
00:24:55,092 --> 00:24:56,642
Big runs are fragile.

421
00:24:56,642 --> 00:25:00,842
The loss can suddenly spike, and sometimes it never recovers.

422
00:25:00,842 --> 00:25:04,992
Google's PaLM saw about twenty spikes during training, even with gradient

423
00:25:04,992 --> 00:25:05,867
clipping;

424
00:25:05,867 --> 00:25:09,954
the fix was to restart from a checkpoint about a hundred steps earlier and

425
00:25:09,954 --> 00:25:12,367
skip a few hundred batches of data.

426
00:25:12,367 --> 00:25:17,042
Meta's OPT needed more than thirty-five manual restarts.

427
00:25:17,042 --> 00:25:20,154
And labs still hit this in 2026:

428
00:25:20,154 --> 00:25:25,592
NVIDIA's Nemotron 3 Ultra report describes two divergences, one traced to

429
00:25:25,592 --> 00:25:30,942
gradients being summed in sixteen-bit instead of thirty-two-bit.

430
00:25:31,442 --> 00:25:34,479
Many instabilities can be reproduced in miniature.

431
00:25:34,479 --> 00:25:38,817
Researchers at Google showed in 2023 that small models trained at high learning

432
00:25:38,817 --> 00:25:41,629
rates fail in the same ways big ones do.

433
00:25:41,629 --> 00:25:43,817
So we trained twelve pocket models:

434
00:25:43,817 --> 00:25:49,004
six learning rates, each with standard attention and with a fix called QK-norm.

435
00:25:49,004 --> 00:25:52,192
These runs are short, so nothing blows up outright.

436
00:25:52,192 --> 00:25:53,842
But look at the shape.

437
00:25:53,842 --> 00:26:01,017
From 0.003 to 0.03, the QK-norm model's loss barely moves, while the standard

438
00:26:01,017 --> 00:26:02,592
model's climbs.

439
00:26:02,592 --> 00:26:06,979
QK-norm makes training far less sensitive to the learning rate, for a small

440
00:26:06,979 --> 00:26:10,067
cost at the best setting.

441
00:26:10,500 --> 00:26:12,062
What's going on inside?

442
00:26:12,062 --> 00:26:15,887
Here is the largest attention score each model produced, the dot product of

443
00:26:15,887 --> 00:26:18,712
a query and a key, against the learning rate.

444
00:26:18,712 --> 00:26:20,837
In the standard model it explodes:

445
00:26:20,837 --> 00:26:25,837
about nine at the gentlest rate, over a hundred at 0.01, and ten thousand

446
00:26:25,837 --> 00:26:27,475
at 0.1.

447
00:26:27,475 --> 00:26:32,050
A softmax over scores that large is one-hot, and in longer runs, this is what

448
00:26:32,050 --> 00:26:35,825
Wortsman and colleagues saw right before the loss diverged.

449
00:26:35,825 --> 00:26:40,762
QK-norm normalizes every query and key vector before the dot product, and

450
00:26:40,762 --> 00:26:45,925
the largest score stays below ten at every learning rate.

451
00:26:46,358 --> 00:26:49,646
QK-norm is now standard in many open models.

452
00:26:49,646 --> 00:26:50,821
Alongside it:

453
00:26:50,821 --> 00:26:55,371
a small z-loss that keeps the output logits from drifting, gradient clipping,

454
00:26:55,371 --> 00:26:57,883
warmup and careful initialization.

455
00:26:57,883 --> 00:27:02,108
Moonshot's MuonClip rescales the query and key weights whenever an attention

456
00:27:02,108 --> 00:27:06,633
score passes a hundred, and Kimi K2 trained on fifteen and a half trillion

457
00:27:06,633 --> 00:27:09,183
tokens with zero loss spikes.

458
00:27:09,183 --> 00:27:11,671
And new failure modes keep appearing:

459
00:27:11,671 --> 00:27:17,633
in 2026, DeepSeek-V4 traced spikes to outliers in its mixture of experts,

460
00:27:17,633 --> 00:27:22,183
and added activation clamping and an emergency re-routing mode that switches

461
00:27:22,183 --> 00:27:23,533
on after a spike.

462
00:27:25,566 --> 00:27:26,253
Part three:

463
00:27:26,253 --> 00:27:28,741
scaling out.

464
00:27:29,716 --> 00:27:32,566
Let's count what a training run has to keep in memory.

465
00:27:32,566 --> 00:27:33,891
For every parameter:

466
00:27:33,891 --> 00:27:36,616
the weight itself, in sixteen-bit, two bytes.

467
00:27:36,616 --> 00:27:37,428
Its gradient:

468
00:27:37,428 --> 00:27:38,353
two more.

469
00:27:38,353 --> 00:27:40,128
Then the optimizer's state:

470
00:27:40,128 --> 00:27:44,141
a full-precision master copy of the weight, four bytes, and Adam's two running

471
00:27:44,141 --> 00:27:46,253
averages, four bytes each.

472
00:27:46,253 --> 00:27:49,566
Sixteen bytes per parameter.

473
00:27:49,991 --> 00:27:56,203
For a 405-billion-parameter model like Llama 3.1, that's six and a half terabytes.

474
00:27:56,203 --> 00:27:58,653
An H100 has eighty gigabytes;

475
00:27:58,653 --> 00:28:02,191
even the newest Blackwell Ultra has 288.

476
00:28:02,191 --> 00:28:06,503
So just holding the training state takes eighty-one H100s, before counting

477
00:28:06,503 --> 00:28:07,941
the activations:

478
00:28:07,941 --> 00:28:11,741
the intermediate values saved during the forward pass for use in the backward

479
00:28:11,741 --> 00:28:15,666
pass, which for long sequences can be even larger.

480
00:28:15,666 --> 00:28:19,591
They're often thrown away and recomputed when needed, trading compute for

481
00:28:19,591 --> 00:28:21,816
memory.

482
00:28:23,257 --> 00:28:27,382
So the work has to be split across many GPUs, and there are several ways to

483
00:28:27,382 --> 00:28:28,370
split it.

484
00:28:28,370 --> 00:28:31,020
The simplest is data parallelism:

485
00:28:31,020 --> 00:28:35,632
every GPU holds a full copy of the model and processes a different slice of

486
00:28:35,632 --> 00:28:36,870
each batch.

487
00:28:36,870 --> 00:28:40,957
Each computes its own gradient, and then they must all agree on the average

488
00:28:40,957 --> 00:28:44,207
before taking a step together.

489
00:28:44,691 --> 00:28:48,828
That averaging is called an all-reduce, and the standard algorithm passes

490
00:28:48,828 --> 00:28:50,703
messages around a ring.

491
00:28:50,703 --> 00:28:53,753
Each GPU's gradient is cut into chunks;

492
00:28:53,753 --> 00:28:56,878
here, four GPUs with four numbers each.

493
00:28:56,878 --> 00:28:59,982
Every GPU needs to end up with the sum.

494
00:28:59,982 --> 00:29:04,282
In each step, every GPU sends one chunk to its neighbor, which adds it to

495
00:29:04,282 --> 00:29:05,320
its own.

496
00:29:05,320 --> 00:29:09,732
After three steps, each GPU holds one chunk of the complete sum.

497
00:29:09,732 --> 00:29:14,170
Three more steps pass the finished chunks around the ring, and now every GPU

498
00:29:14,170 --> 00:29:15,645
has the whole sum.

499
00:29:15,645 --> 00:29:20,020
Each one sent only about twice the size of its gradient, no matter how many

500
00:29:20,020 --> 00:29:23,032
GPUs are in the ring.

501
00:29:23,516 --> 00:29:27,941
But in plain data parallelism, every GPU still stores all sixteen bytes per

502
00:29:27,941 --> 00:29:28,966
parameter.

503
00:29:28,966 --> 00:29:33,191
ZeRO, and PyTorch's FSDP, shard that state instead.

504
00:29:33,191 --> 00:29:38,441
For a 7.5-billion-parameter model on sixty-four GPUs, that's 120 gigabytes

505
00:29:38,441 --> 00:29:39,616
per GPU.

506
00:29:39,616 --> 00:29:43,841
Split the optimizer state sixty-four ways, and it drops to 31.

507
00:29:43,841 --> 00:29:45,328
Shard the gradients too:

508
00:29:45,328 --> 00:29:46,541
17.

509
00:29:46,541 --> 00:29:50,416
Shard the weights themselves, gathering each layer just before it's needed:

510
00:29:50,416 --> 00:29:52,266
under two gigabytes.

511
00:29:52,266 --> 00:29:54,578
The price is more communication:

512
00:29:54,578 --> 00:29:58,016
about one and a half times that of plain data parallelism.

513
00:30:00,071 --> 00:30:04,321
Data parallelism still runs every layer in full on every GPU.

514
00:30:04,321 --> 00:30:07,733
When a single layer is too big, you split the layer itself.

515
00:30:07,733 --> 00:30:09,608
That's tensor parallelism.

516
00:30:09,608 --> 00:30:11,658
Take a matrix multiplication.

517
00:30:11,658 --> 00:30:15,833
Cut the weight matrix into columns, and each GPU computes its own slice of

518
00:30:15,833 --> 00:30:18,446
the output, with no communication at all.

519
00:30:18,446 --> 00:30:23,158
Feed those slices into the next matrix, cut into rows, and each GPU produces

520
00:30:23,158 --> 00:30:24,471
a partial sum.

521
00:30:24,471 --> 00:30:27,721
One all-reduce adds them up.

522
00:30:28,217 --> 00:30:32,854
NVIDIA's Megatron-LM arranged a whole transformer layer this way, with just

523
00:30:32,854 --> 00:30:36,492
two all-reduces in the forward pass and two in the backward.

524
00:30:36,492 --> 00:30:40,842
But they happen in every layer, on the critical path, so tensor parallelism

525
00:30:40,842 --> 00:30:46,742
is kept inside one server, where eight GPUs are joined by NVLink at 450 gigabytes

526
00:30:46,742 --> 00:30:48,954
per second in each direction.

527
00:30:48,954 --> 00:30:52,567
Between servers, a network card manages about 50:

528
00:30:52,567 --> 00:30:55,467
nine times slower.

529
00:30:55,917 --> 00:30:59,504
To spread a model across servers, cut it by depth instead:

530
00:30:59,504 --> 00:31:01,367
pipeline parallelism.

531
00:31:01,367 --> 00:31:05,617
The first GPU holds the first quarter of the layers, the next GPU the next

532
00:31:05,617 --> 00:31:07,379
quarter, and so on.

533
00:31:07,379 --> 00:31:11,542
Only activations pass between stages, so slower links are fine.

534
00:31:11,542 --> 00:31:14,204
But a naive pipeline is mostly idle.

535
00:31:14,204 --> 00:31:16,504
While one stage works, the others wait:

536
00:31:16,504 --> 00:31:19,108
three quarters of the time is bubble.

537
00:31:19,108 --> 00:31:23,558
The fix is to cut each batch into micro-batches and keep the pipeline full.

538
00:31:23,558 --> 00:31:28,083
With eight micro-batches, the bubble shrinks to p minus one, over m plus p

539
00:31:28,083 --> 00:31:29,071
minus one:

540
00:31:29,071 --> 00:31:30,396
about a quarter.

541
00:31:30,396 --> 00:31:32,608
Cleverer schedules push it further.

542
00:31:32,608 --> 00:31:36,946
Zero-bubble pipelines split the backward pass in two and use one half to fill

543
00:31:36,946 --> 00:31:41,208
the gaps, and DeepSeek's DualPipe feeds micro-batches in from both ends at

544
00:31:41,208 --> 00:31:45,958
once, hiding communication behind computation.

545
00:31:46,442 --> 00:31:49,929
Mixture-of-experts models add expert parallelism.

546
00:31:49,929 --> 00:31:54,667
Each GPU holds different experts, and every token must travel to the GPUs

547
00:31:54,667 --> 00:31:57,767
holding its chosen experts, and back again:

548
00:31:57,767 --> 00:32:01,567
an all-to-all exchange in every MoE layer.

549
00:32:01,567 --> 00:32:06,292
And for very long sequences, context parallelism splits the sequence itself

550
00:32:06,292 --> 00:32:09,192
across GPUs.

551
00:32:09,683 --> 00:32:11,921
Real runs combine all of these.

552
00:32:11,921 --> 00:32:19,183
Llama 3.1, the 405-billion-parameter model, trained on 16,384 H100s arranged

553
00:32:19,183 --> 00:32:20,196
as a grid:

554
00:32:20,196 --> 00:32:24,583
tensor parallel across the eight GPUs inside each server, a sixteen-stage

555
00:32:24,583 --> 00:32:28,408
pipeline across sixteen servers, and a hundred and twenty-eight copies of

556
00:32:28,408 --> 00:32:31,121
that pipeline doing data parallelism.

557
00:32:31,121 --> 00:32:34,521
Each GPU sustained about 400 teraflops:

558
00:32:34,521 --> 00:32:38,058
roughly forty percent of its peak, which is typical.

559
00:32:38,058 --> 00:32:44,383
DeepSeek-V3 took a different shape, on 2,048 export-limited H800s:

560
00:32:44,383 --> 00:32:49,783
no tensor parallelism at all, a sixteen-stage DualPipe pipeline, and experts

561
00:32:49,783 --> 00:32:52,808
spread across 64 GPUs on eight nodes.

562
00:32:54,877 --> 00:32:59,302
A run like this lasts weeks or months, and keeping it alive is a job in itself,

563
00:32:59,302 --> 00:33:03,002
because the more GPUs you use, the more often something breaks.

564
00:33:03,002 --> 00:33:05,577
Meta measured this across its research clusters:

565
00:33:05,577 --> 00:33:10,065
an eight-GPU job ran, on average, forty-eight days between failures.

566
00:33:10,065 --> 00:33:11,777
A thousand-GPU job:

567
00:33:11,777 --> 00:33:13,327
about eight hours.

568
00:33:13,327 --> 00:33:17,415
Failures scale with the number of parts, so at sixteen thousand GPUs you'd

569
00:33:17,415 --> 00:33:21,790
expect one every couple of hours, and at a hundred thousand, about every quarter

570
00:33:21,790 --> 00:33:24,077
of an hour.

571
00:33:24,519 --> 00:33:26,581
And that's what Llama 3 saw.

572
00:33:26,581 --> 00:33:33,294
In one fifty-four-day stretch of training on 16,384 GPUs, the job was interrupted

573
00:33:33,294 --> 00:33:35,531
466 times:

574
00:33:35,531 --> 00:33:37,806
once every two point eight hours.

575
00:33:37,806 --> 00:33:42,669
About four fifths of the unexpected stops traced to hardware, mostly the GPUs

576
00:33:42,669 --> 00:33:45,969
themselves and their memory.

577
00:33:46,519 --> 00:33:49,244
So the training state is checkpointed regularly.

578
00:33:49,244 --> 00:33:53,919
When a GPU dies, the job restarts from the last checkpoint on healthy machines,

579
00:33:53,919 --> 00:33:56,156
and only the work since then is lost.

580
00:33:56,156 --> 00:33:59,606
Doing this well is the difference between a run that spends nine tenths of

581
00:33:59,606 --> 00:34:02,269
its time training and one that doesn't.

582
00:34:02,269 --> 00:34:04,969
Llama 3 stayed above ninety percent.

583
00:34:04,969 --> 00:34:09,369
Google kept redundant copies of Gemini's state in memory, so recovery took

584
00:34:09,369 --> 00:34:13,669
seconds, and raised the share of useful time from eighty-five to ninety-seven

585
00:34:13,669 --> 00:34:15,894
percent.

586
00:34:16,377 --> 00:34:18,090
Some failures are silent:

587
00:34:18,090 --> 00:34:21,652
a chip that keeps running but computes slightly wrong numbers.

588
00:34:21,652 --> 00:34:25,990
At Gemini's scale, Google expected such silent data corruption every week

589
00:34:25,990 --> 00:34:28,890
or two, and caught it by replaying steps.

590
00:34:28,890 --> 00:34:30,852
Even the power grid notices:

591
00:34:30,852 --> 00:34:35,640
when sixteen thousand GPUs pause at the same moment, Meta saw the power draw

592
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:37,577
swing by tens of megawatts.

593
00:34:39,692 --> 00:34:40,317
Part four:

594
00:34:40,317 --> 00:34:42,867
post-training.

595
00:34:43,817 --> 00:34:46,330
After pretraining, we have a base model:

596
00:34:46,330 --> 00:34:49,717
a superb document completer, but not an assistant.

597
00:34:49,717 --> 00:34:54,792
Ask the base version of Qwen3's smallest model where the Eiffel Tower is,

598
00:34:54,792 --> 00:34:57,017
and it decides it's reading a quiz.

599
00:34:57,017 --> 00:35:01,717
It writes the multiple-choice options, the answer key, and then the next question.

600
00:35:01,717 --> 00:35:06,292
The same model after post-training simply answers.

601
00:35:06,892 --> 00:35:09,655
The first step is supervised fine-tuning.

602
00:35:09,655 --> 00:35:13,942
Conversations are written out as a single stream of tokens, with special tokens

603
00:35:13,942 --> 00:35:15,680
marking who is speaking.

604
00:35:15,680 --> 00:35:18,255
Here is Qwen3's actual format:

605
00:35:18,255 --> 00:35:22,505
a start marker, the speaker's role, the message, and an end marker.

606
00:35:22,505 --> 00:35:26,692
The assistant's turn even begins with a thinking block, empty here because

607
00:35:26,692 --> 00:35:28,751
thinking was switched off.

608
00:35:28,751 --> 00:35:33,376
Training is the same next-token prediction as before, with one change:

609
00:35:33,376 --> 00:35:36,663
the loss is only counted on the assistant's tokens.

610
00:35:36,663 --> 00:35:39,651
The model isn't learning to predict what users say.

611
00:35:39,651 --> 00:35:43,551
It's learning how the assistant replies.

612
00:35:44,059 --> 00:35:46,022
Where do the conversations come from?

613
00:35:46,022 --> 00:35:47,947
Early on, from people:

614
00:35:47,947 --> 00:35:53,309
OpenAI's InstructGPT used about thirteen thousand demonstrations written by

615
00:35:53,309 --> 00:35:54,747
contractors.

616
00:35:54,747 --> 00:35:59,009
The LIMA paper showed that a thousand carefully chosen examples go a long

617
00:35:59,009 --> 00:36:03,059
way, because the knowledge is already there from pretraining.

618
00:36:03,059 --> 00:36:07,834
Today most of the data is generated by stronger models and then filtered:

619
00:36:07,834 --> 00:36:14,159
Allen AI's Tülu 3 mix has 939 thousand prompts, and OLMo 3's reasoning set

620
00:36:14,159 --> 00:36:18,622
has 2.3 million, about forty-five billion tokens.

621
00:36:18,622 --> 00:36:21,409
This kind of imitation is powerful.

622
00:36:21,409 --> 00:36:26,872
DeepSeek distilled R1's reasoning into a 32-billion-parameter Qwen model with

623
00:36:26,872 --> 00:36:33,909
fine-tuning alone, and it scored 72.6 percent on the AIME 2024 math competition,

624
00:36:33,909 --> 00:36:38,672
far above the 47 percent that reinforcement learning reached directly on the

625
00:36:38,672 --> 00:36:39,684
same base.

626
00:36:41,816 --> 00:36:44,141
Imitation only goes so far.

627
00:36:44,141 --> 00:36:48,353
For many qualities, it's far easier to judge an answer than to write a perfect

628
00:36:48,353 --> 00:36:49,141
one.

629
00:36:49,141 --> 00:36:51,978
So the next stage learns from comparisons:

630
00:36:51,978 --> 00:36:58,216
show a person two responses to the same prompt, and ask which one is better.

631
00:36:58,707 --> 00:37:01,270
Those choices train a reward model:

632
00:37:01,270 --> 00:37:06,107
a copy of the language model whose output is a single number, a score.

633
00:37:06,107 --> 00:37:10,407
It's trained so that the probability a person prefers answer A over answer

634
00:37:10,407 --> 00:37:14,095
B is the sigmoid of the difference between their scores.

635
00:37:14,095 --> 00:37:18,757
That's the Bradley-Terry model, from 1952.

636
00:37:19,182 --> 00:37:21,307
Then comes reinforcement learning.

637
00:37:21,307 --> 00:37:25,582
The model writes answers, the reward model scores them, and the model is nudged

638
00:37:25,582 --> 00:37:29,145
toward the answers that score higher, with one constraint:

639
00:37:29,145 --> 00:37:32,995
a penalty for drifting too far from the fine-tuned model it started from,

640
00:37:32,995 --> 00:37:35,432
measured by the KL divergence.

641
00:37:35,432 --> 00:37:39,882
This is RLHF, reinforcement learning from human feedback:

642
00:37:39,882 --> 00:37:45,457
the recipe behind InstructGPT and the first ChatGPT.

643
00:37:45,916 --> 00:37:49,516
The leash matters, because the reward model is only a proxy for what people

644
00:37:49,516 --> 00:37:50,228
want.

645
00:37:50,228 --> 00:37:51,803
Here's a simulation.

646
00:37:51,803 --> 00:37:56,091
Each candidate answer has a true quality, and the reward model sees that quality

647
00:37:56,091 --> 00:37:57,428
plus some error.

648
00:37:57,428 --> 00:38:00,791
Pick the best of n answers by the reward model's score.

649
00:38:00,791 --> 00:38:03,191
At first, true quality climbs.

650
00:38:03,191 --> 00:38:07,516
But push harder, and you start selecting the answers whose errors were biggest:

651
00:38:07,516 --> 00:38:11,441
the proxy reward keeps rising while the true quality falls.

652
00:38:11,441 --> 00:38:16,416
That's Goodhart's law, and OpenAI measured exactly this shape with real reward

653
00:38:16,416 --> 00:38:19,641
models in 2022.

654
00:38:20,082 --> 00:38:25,232
In 2023, Direct Preference Optimization showed you can skip the reward model

655
00:38:25,232 --> 00:38:27,470
and the RL loop altogether.

656
00:38:27,470 --> 00:38:32,045
A bit of algebra turns the same objective into a simple loss on each pair:

657
00:38:32,045 --> 00:38:36,320
raise the probability of the preferred answer, relative to the reference model,

658
00:38:36,320 --> 00:38:38,407
and lower the rejected one.

659
00:38:38,407 --> 00:38:43,607
DPO and its variants are now standard in open post-training pipelines.

660
00:38:43,607 --> 00:38:46,482
And the comparisons don't have to come from people:

661
00:38:46,482 --> 00:38:51,282
with constitutional AI, a model judges responses against a written list of

662
00:38:51,282 --> 00:38:52,182
principles.

663
00:38:54,289 --> 00:38:58,789
The biggest change in post-training since 2024 is reinforcement learning on

664
00:38:58,789 --> 00:39:02,102
problems whose answers can be checked automatically.

665
00:39:02,102 --> 00:39:04,527
A math problem has a final answer;

666
00:39:04,527 --> 00:39:06,077
code has tests.

667
00:39:06,077 --> 00:39:10,752
The reward is simply one if the answer is right, and zero if it isn't.

668
00:39:10,752 --> 00:39:15,264
There's no learned reward model for the policy to fool.

669
00:39:15,714 --> 00:39:21,764
Here's the most popular algorithm for this, GRPO, run for real on a miniature.

670
00:39:21,764 --> 00:39:25,702
We pretrained a pocket model on three-digit additions, where four in ten of

671
00:39:25,702 --> 00:39:29,564
the examples were written by someone who always forgets to carry.

672
00:39:29,564 --> 00:39:31,589
So the model learned two habits:

673
00:39:31,589 --> 00:39:35,964
the right answer, and the carry-dropping mistake.

674
00:39:36,414 --> 00:39:41,952
Give it a problem, 478 plus 356, and sample a group of eight answers.

675
00:39:41,952 --> 00:39:43,039
Only one is right:

676
00:39:43,039 --> 00:39:44,527
834.

677
00:39:44,527 --> 00:39:48,527
Five drop both carries, 724, and two drop one of them.

678
00:39:48,527 --> 00:39:50,002
Each answer gets a reward:

679
00:39:50,002 --> 00:39:51,339
one or zero.

680
00:39:51,339 --> 00:39:55,577
GRPO's key idea is to grade each answer against its own group:

681
00:39:55,577 --> 00:39:59,189
subtract the group's average reward, and divide by the spread.

682
00:39:59,189 --> 00:40:01,927
Better than average gets a positive advantage;

683
00:40:01,927 --> 00:40:04,114
worse gets a negative one.

684
00:40:04,114 --> 00:40:08,464
Then every token of each answer is pushed up or down in probability according

685
00:40:08,464 --> 00:40:10,014
to its advantage.

686
00:40:10,014 --> 00:40:12,877
No critic network, no reward model:

687
00:40:12,877 --> 00:40:16,464
just a group of samples and a checker.

688
00:40:16,914 --> 00:40:19,352
Repeat that over thousands of problems.

689
00:40:19,352 --> 00:40:23,864
Pass at one, the chance that a single sample is right, climbs from about sixty

690
00:40:23,864 --> 00:40:25,889
percent to nearly a hundred.

691
00:40:25,889 --> 00:40:29,339
But look at pass at eight, the chance that at least one of eight samples is

692
00:40:29,339 --> 00:40:30,089
right.

693
00:40:30,089 --> 00:40:31,339
It barely moves:

694
00:40:31,339 --> 00:40:33,164
it was already near the top.

695
00:40:33,164 --> 00:40:37,239
In this toy, reinforcement learning didn't teach the model anything new.

696
00:40:37,239 --> 00:40:40,714
It made it reliable at what it could already sometimes do.

697
00:40:40,714 --> 00:40:43,877
Whether that also holds at the frontier is debated:

698
00:40:43,877 --> 00:40:49,039
a 2025 study found the same pattern in large models, while others find that

699
00:40:49,039 --> 00:40:53,281
prolonged RL discovers genuinely new strategies.

700
00:40:53,281 --> 00:40:55,618
And notice what happens late in training:

701
00:40:55,618 --> 00:41:01,593
most groups are all correct, like our 478 plus 356, which now gets eight out

702
00:41:01,593 --> 00:41:02,543
of eight.

703
00:41:02,543 --> 00:41:06,143
Every advantage is zero, and those groups teach nothing.

704
00:41:06,143 --> 00:41:10,581
Refinements like DAPO's dynamic sampling filter them out, alongside other

705
00:41:10,581 --> 00:41:15,281
tweaks to the clipping and to how the loss is averaged.

706
00:41:15,689 --> 00:41:19,039
At frontier scale, this is how reasoning models are made.

707
00:41:19,039 --> 00:41:23,589
DeepSeek-R1-Zero applied it directly to a base model, with just two rewards:

708
00:41:23,589 --> 00:41:26,114
is the answer correct, and is the format right?

709
00:41:26,114 --> 00:41:31,939
Its score on the AIME 2024 math competition rose from 15.6 percent to 71.

710
00:41:31,939 --> 00:41:35,464
Its answers grew from hundreds of tokens to thousands, as it learned on its

711
00:41:35,464 --> 00:41:37,152
own to re-check its work.

712
00:41:37,152 --> 00:41:39,614
In one transcript it stops and writes:

713
00:41:39,614 --> 00:41:40,964
wait, wait.

714
00:41:40,964 --> 00:41:42,114
Wait.

715
00:41:42,114 --> 00:41:45,789
That's an aha moment I can flag here.

716
00:41:46,348 --> 00:41:48,773
Today's frontier RL goes further:

717
00:41:48,773 --> 00:41:52,635
long, multi-step tasks with tools, in sandboxes.

718
00:41:52,635 --> 00:41:56,973
Fixing real software issues, driving a terminal, browsing the web.

719
00:41:56,973 --> 00:42:01,835
DeepSeek-V3.2 synthesized more than eighteen hundred environments, and spent

720
00:42:01,835 --> 00:42:05,448
over ten percent of its pretraining compute on post-training.

721
00:42:05,448 --> 00:42:08,660
Generating those long rollouts dominates the cost:

722
00:42:08,660 --> 00:42:12,823
in OLMo 3's runs, the learner spent three quarters of its time waiting for

723
00:42:12,823 --> 00:42:13,998
data.

724
00:42:13,998 --> 00:42:19,248
So generation and training run on separate GPUs, asynchronously, with fresh

725
00:42:19,248 --> 00:42:23,648
weights streamed to the generators as training goes.

726
00:42:24,156 --> 00:42:26,906
And reinforcement learning finds loopholes.

727
00:42:26,906 --> 00:42:31,531
If a test can be gamed, a model will eventually game it, like a prime checker

728
00:42:31,531 --> 00:42:34,356
that simply memorizes the test cases.

729
00:42:34,356 --> 00:42:39,193
Anthropic reported in 2025 that models which learned to cheat in real coding

730
00:42:39,193 --> 00:42:42,831
environments generalized to broader bad behavior.

731
00:42:42,831 --> 00:42:47,231
Building environments that can't be gamed is now a central part of the job.

732
00:42:49,412 --> 00:42:51,912
Before release, the model is evaluated:

733
00:42:51,912 --> 00:42:56,524
on hard benchmarks in science, math and coding, on long agentic tasks, and

734
00:42:56,524 --> 00:43:00,012
on safety tests, all written up in a system card.

735
00:43:00,012 --> 00:43:02,012
Benchmarks are a moving target:

736
00:43:02,012 --> 00:43:05,124
they saturate, and they leak into training data.

737
00:43:05,124 --> 00:43:10,962
In February 2026, OpenAI stopped reporting the popular SWE-bench Verified

738
00:43:10,962 --> 00:43:14,137
because of contamination.

739
00:43:14,603 --> 00:43:16,628
So here is the whole pipeline.

740
00:43:16,628 --> 00:43:21,166
Billions of crawled pages, filtered and deduplicated down to tens of trillions

741
00:43:21,166 --> 00:43:22,416
of tokens.

742
00:43:22,416 --> 00:43:27,291
A recipe chosen with scaling laws, today usually a sparse mixture of experts,

743
00:43:27,291 --> 00:43:32,216
trained with Muon or AdamW in eight- or even four-bit arithmetic.

744
00:43:32,216 --> 00:43:37,353
Tens of thousands of GPUs, coordinated by four kinds of parallelism, and kept

745
00:43:37,353 --> 00:43:40,453
alive through failures every few hours.

746
00:43:40,453 --> 00:43:44,653
Then fine-tuning, preferences and reinforcement learning, which now takes

747
00:43:44,653 --> 00:43:47,028
a substantial share of the compute.

748
00:43:47,028 --> 00:43:50,403
And finally, the assistant.

749
00:43:50,987 --> 00:43:52,562
And our pocket versions?

750
00:43:52,562 --> 00:43:56,712
The largest model we trained had about three million parameters, against up

751
00:43:56,712 --> 00:43:59,449
to 2.8 trillion at the frontier.

752
00:43:59,449 --> 00:44:03,787
All of our training runs together took less than ten to the fifteen operations;

753
00:44:03,787 --> 00:44:08,274
the largest frontier run is estimated at around ten to the twenty-seven.

754
00:44:08,274 --> 00:44:12,474
Four CPU cores, against a hundred thousand GPUs.

755
00:44:12,474 --> 00:44:16,112
Yet the curves have the same shapes.

756
00:44:16,770 --> 00:44:20,645
Almost everything in this video comes from open-weight labs and open-source

757
00:44:20,645 --> 00:44:21,695
projects;

758
00:44:21,695 --> 00:44:26,120
the leading closed labs publish very little about how their models are trained.

759
00:44:26,120 --> 00:44:29,457
But the shape of the pipeline has been stable for years:

760
00:44:29,457 --> 00:44:34,120
get the data right, choose the size with scaling laws, keep the run stable

761
00:44:34,120 --> 00:44:38,320
across thousands of machines, and then teach the model with feedback.

762
00:44:38,320 --> 00:44:40,895
And more of it is open than ever.

763
00:44:40,895 --> 00:44:45,057
AI2's OLMo 3 releases every dataset and checkpoint;

764
00:44:45,057 --> 00:44:50,082
nanochat trains a GPT-2-grade chat model for under a hundred dollars;

765
00:44:50,082 --> 00:44:53,545
Hugging Face's playbooks write down the hard-won lessons.

766
00:44:53,545 --> 00:44:57,045
If you want to know how these models are made, you can run the whole thing

767
00:44:57,045 --> 00:44:59,470
yourself, at whatever scale you have.
